## Base template

Dear [HIRING TEAM | NAMED CONTACT],

I would like to apply for the [ROLE TITLE] role [in LOCATION | at COMPANY]. I bring product manager experience [PICK ONE OR COMBINE: from end-to-end ownership of a live multi-tenant SaaS | from a national platform strategy at the C-suite of a $100M+ turnover organisation | already exercised at the national level of a (DOMAIN — e.g. mass-participation Australian sport)], all underpinned by a strong technical understanding from my engineering background.

My product work spans discovery and stakeholder engagement, opportunity sizing and business cases, roadmap and prioritisation, and the cross-functional delivery that turns strategy into shipped product. I act as product manager for LIMSOC, a mission-critical incident management system used across the country. My most recent engagement was an independent consulting piece authoring the platform's national scaling strategy and presenting it directly to the national executive. [PICK ONE BASED ON JD: That work covered stakeholder requirements, product documentation, roadmap and national vision, and the testing and release coordination that goes with it. | That work covered discovery across the federation, opportunity sizing for a national investment, and the business case to support it.] Across Life Saving Victoria and Surf Life Saving Australia I have played a pivotal role in reshaping how those organisations approach operational technology, with appropriate governance, stakeholder engagement, and fit-for-purpose outcomes. [OPTIONAL — include only if the JD specifically values multi-stakeholder governance: I also led a national review on the policy at the core of Surf Life Saving Australia's purpose, our water safety policy, bringing every state into the room and into agreement on the foundations to continue improving towards our vision.]

My time as a scrum master scaled the practice from two squads to six, so agile ceremonies, blocker removal, and predictable cadence are practiced not theoretical. Writing high-quality user stories, acceptance criteria, and business requirements is everyday work for me, and I know what good looks like from both sides of the fence, from years as the engineer delivering against them before writing them. Alongside that, I designed and shipped AccessBuddy, a multi-tenant SaaS live in production with real users, running as a solo founder at the velocity of a small team. I owned it end to end, from schema and API design through to OAuth and integration patterns, GCP deployment, observability, and the customer-facing product itself.

All of this is underpinned by an engineering background, most recently on ANZ's generative AI platform building AI-enabled search APIs in Vertex AI and Gemini. That depth lets me comfortably hold both business strategy and technical conversations, and translating between the two is where I add the most leverage. [OPTIONAL — include if the JD calls for technical depth or systems thinking: It also brings transferable disciplines, breaking complex problems into structured pieces, weighing trade-offs around scale and reliability, designing APIs for the long run, and instrumenting systems so the data tells the truth.]

What I want now is the broader scope and impact the product seat gives, and [COMPANY] is where I want to do that. [COMPANY-SPECIFIC HOOK: 2-3 sentences pulling concrete from the JD or company — scale signal with specific numbers, mission framing, or a JD-skills mapping. End with why this role specifically fits the next step.]

I look forward to the opportunity to discuss the role further with you.

Nicholas Wood
